## Why This Is Asked
To evaluate ability to design end-to-end retrieval systems with multilingual data, latency constraints, and production-grade maintenance. The candidate must articulate chunking, embeddings, indexing, reranking, provenance, and monitoring trade-offs in a realistic enterprise setting.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation
- Embeddings for multilingual data
- Vector databases and indexing strategies (IVF+PQ, HNSW)
- Document chunking and provenance
- Reranking with cross-encoders
- Drifts, updates, caching, latency budgets
- Evaluation metrics: recall@k, QA accuracy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: initial retrieval then rerank
function retrieveAnswer(query, docs){
  const chunks = chunkDocs(docs);
  const emb = embed(query);
  const candidates = vectorDB.search(emb, topK=10);
  const ranked = reranker.rank(query, candidates);
  return formatAnswer(ranked[0]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor embedding drift across languages?
- How would you handle adding a new language with few examples?
- What metrics would you use to balance latency vs. accuracy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical skills designing a rigorous evaluation framework for a multilingual policy assistant that must ensure every factual claim maps to a live source while detecting cross-source conflicts and tracking policy drift over time.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual policy alignment and factual consistency
- Versioned source management and drift detection
- Cross-source hallucination detection for conflicting information
- Automated evaluation with comprehensive metrics and tooling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal verification framework: map claims to source citations
function verifyClaim(claim, sources) {
  const citation = findSourceCitation(claim, sources);
  return {
    claim,
    source: citation.url,
    timestamp: citation.version,
    isValid: validateClaim(claim, citation)
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of a full retrieval pipeline, chunking strategy, and vector stores in a beginner-friendly way applicable to large-prod settings (Lyft/Discord/Adobe).

## Key Concepts

- Token-based chunking and overlap
- Local embeddings vs cloud APIs
- Vector databases and cosine similarity
- Data freshness and refresh strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal in-memory demo (no real model)
const chunks = [
  { text: 'chunk one', vec: [0.1,0.2,0.3] },
  { text: 'chunk two', vec: [0.0,0.2,0.4] }
];
function sim(a,b){ let dot = a.reduce((s,x,i)=> s + x*b[i],0); let na=Math.hypot(...a); let nb=Math.hypot(...b); return dot/(na*nb); }
const q=[0.08,0.18,0.28];
const top = chunks.map((c,i)=>({i, s: sim(c.vec,q)})).sort((A,B)=> B.s-A.s)[0];
console.log(top);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle chunking for languages with complex morphology?
- How would you test for model drift in embeddings over time?
